<content><![CDATA[<p>Check your understanding of CNC routing. Your answers aren't recorded.</p><multiple-choice question="In 2.5D milling, what can't a straight end mill cut, whatever the toolpath?" single-option="" randomize="">
  <input type="checkbox" value="An inside corner sharper than the bit's radius" data-correct="true">
  <input type="checkbox" value="A pocket deeper than half the stock">
  <input type="checkbox" value="A curved outline">
  <input type="checkbox" value="Lettering">
</multiple-choice><multiple-choice question="What are tabs for in a profile cut?" single-option="" randomize="">
  <input type="checkbox" value="They hold the part to the stock so it can't shift or fly loose as the cut finishes" data-correct="true">
  <input type="checkbox" value="They mark where the cut starts">
  <input type="checkbox" value="They cool the bit">
  <input type="checkbox" value="They stop tear-out on the top face">
</multiple-choice><true-false-question question="Feeds and speeds only change how long a job takes, not the quality or safety of the cut.">
  <input type="checkbox" value="False" data-correct="true">
  <input type="checkbox" value="True">
</true-false-question><self-check title="Think it through">
  <p slot="question">Before you start a CNC job, what do you check about zero?</p>
  <p>That X and Y zero are where the CAM setup expects them (a corner or the centre of the stock), and that Z zero is set to the surface the CAM used (the top of the stock or the spoilboard). A mismatch cuts air, or cuts into the spoilboard.</p>
</self-check>]]></content>
